# Target C — audited §§160–161 retained component remainder and internal-face area slack

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module extracts the live all-regime content from the historical component-geodesicity / area-slack branch. The old scalar endpoint consequences are deliberately not promoted: the conditional `h/D=1.8169968...` bound is weaker than the already audited unconditional §146 floor `1.8195135...`, and the older §148 lower-edge endpoint is therefore no longer a live branch.

The useful surviving structure is stronger because it retains a nonnegative component remainder instead of discarding it.

---

# §160 — exact component remainder and internal-face isoperimetric slack

For each unloaded positive-contact component `I_r`, write

- `delta_r` for its contact-graph diameter;
- `d_r=diam(P_r)` for its loaded attachment-cell diameter;
- `r_r:=2n_r-delta_r-2-e_r>=0` for its Eppstein deficit; and
- `eta_r:=delta_r+2-d_r>=0` for its graph-to-Euclidean diameter slack.

Let

\[
R_I:=\sum_r r_r,
\]

and retain the canonical nonnegative loaded-degree slack `s_B`.

Define

\[
\boxed{
G_D:=2+q+m-k-\sum_r d_r.
}
\tag{160.1}
\]

Then every sufficiently large Type-II survivor satisfies the exact finite identity

\[
\boxed{
G_D
=R_I+s_B+\sum_r\eta_r
\ge0.
}
\tag{160.2}
\]

This identity is an exact refinement of the component-diameter inequality used in the weighted post-123 ledger.

## 160.1 Proof of the exact remainder identity

The canonical component ledger, after the centralized Type-II loaded-contact theorem `c_B=0`, is

\[
\sum_r\delta_r+R_I+s_B
=2+q+m-k-2c_I.
\tag{160.3}
\]

By definition of `eta_r`,

\[
\sum_r d_r
=\sum_r\delta_r+2c_I-\sum_r\eta_r.
\tag{160.4}
\]

Subtract (160.4) from `2+q+m-k` and use (160.3). This gives (160.2) with no limiting argument.

## 160.2 Internal-face formula for one component

Let `L_r^I` be the occurrence-counted outer-face length of the internal plane graph `C[I_r]`, and let

\[
Q_r^I:=\sum_F(\deg F-4)
\]

over its bounded nonquadrilateral faces. Triangle-freeness gives bounded face degree at least four. Euler plus face incidence yields exactly

\[
e_r=2n_r-2-{L_r^I+Q_r^I\over2}.
\tag{160.5}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
r_r={L_r^I+Q_r^I\over2}-\delta_r.}
\tag{160.6}
\]

Since `Q_r^I>=0`,

\[
L_r^I\le2(\delta_r+r_r).
\tag{160.7}
\]

## 160.3 Isoperimetry with bridges and articulation points

For any finite connected straight-line plane graph with unit edges and occurrence-counted outer-face length `L`, take the union of the closures of all bounded faces. Its positive-area connected components may meet at articulation points or other zero-area sets. If their outer boundary lengths are `p_1,...,p_s`, then every physical edge on these outer boundaries contributes at least one occurrence to the graph's outer-face walk, while bridges contribute outer occurrences but no bounded-area boundary. Hence

\[
\sum_jp_j\le L.
\tag{160.8}
\]

Planar isoperimetry gives

\[
\sum_jA_j
\le {1\over4\pi}\sum_jp_j^2
\le {L^2\over4\pi}.
\tag{160.9}
\]

Let `A_{4,r}^{int}` be the total area of strict-rhombus bounded faces whose four vertices lie in `I_r`. These faces lie in the bounded-face union of `C[I_r]`. Combining (160.7)–(160.9),

\[
\boxed{
A_{4,r}^{int}
\le{(\delta_r+r_r)^2\over\pi}.
}
\tag{160.10}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
r_r+\eta_r
\ge\sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{int}}+2-d_r.}
\tag{160.11}
\]

Since `G_D` contains `R_I` and the full sum of `eta_s`,

\[
\boxed{
G_D\ge r_r+\eta_r
\ge\left[\sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{int}}+2-d_r\right]_+.
}
\tag{160.12}
\]

The positive part is the safe all-profile form.

## 160.4 Loaded-touching rhombi are only boundary order

For one harmonic cell, the attachment set has `a_r=O(D)` loaded vertices and every loaded vertex has full contact degree at most three. Thus only `O(D)` strict-rhombus faces of that harmonic cell can touch a loaded attachment. A unit rhombus has area at most one.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
A_{4,r}^{int}
\ge A_{4,r}-O(D).
}
\tag{160.13}
\]

This transfer is useful whenever a macroscopic carrier has a quadratic strict-rhombus area floor.

If along a subsequence

\[
A_{4,r}^{int}\ge(A-o(1))D^2,
\qquad
{d_r\over D}\to\lambda,
\]

then (160.12) gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {G_D\over D}
\ge[\sqrt{\pi A}-\lambda]_+.
}
\tag{160.14}
\]

This is the live geometric content of the historical endpoint-isoperimetry argument.

