# §161 — retained-remainder weighted payment

Normalized §148 gives the audited-working weighted Type-II payment

\[
3q-2m-5k
\ge\sum_r d_r+c_*D-o(D),
\qquad
c_*={\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3).
\tag{161.1}
\]

Solve (160.1) for `m`:

\[
m=G_D+\sum_r d_r-q+k-2.
\tag{161.2}
\]

Substituting into (161.1) gives

\[
5q-7k-2G_D-3\sum_r d_r+4
\ge c_*D-o(D).
\]

Absorb the constant `4` into `o(D)` along an unbounded sequence:

\[
\boxed{
5q-7k
\ge3\sum_r d_r+2G_D+c_*D-o(D).
}
\tag{161.3}
\]

Using

\[
q=h+d_H,
\qquad k=d_H+s_Z,
\]

we obtain the source-level form

\[
\boxed{
5h-2d_H-7s_Z
\ge3\sum_r d_r+2G_D+c_*D-o(D).
}
\tag{161.4}
\]

This strictly refines the same weighted inequality with `G_D` discarded.

## 161.1 Profile specialization

Suppose along a Type-II subsequence

\[
{1\over D}\sum_r d_r\ge S-o(1),
\]

and one selected harmonic component satisfies

\[
A_{4,r}^{int}\ge(A-o(1))D^2,
\qquad
{d_r\over D}\to\lambda.
\]

Then §§160–161 give

\[
\boxed{
5h-2d_H-7s_Z
\ge
\left[
3S+2[\sqrt{\pi A}-\lambda]_++c_*
\right]D-o(D).
}
\tag{161.5}
\]

The carrier and area parameters must refer to the **same** component. No independent optimization of `A` and `lambda` is permitted.

At the historical safe §118 frontier profile,

\[
\lambda_*=0.9762439137425688\ldots,
\qquad
A_*=0.4703752868004241\ldots,
\qquad
S_*=2.798596312209462\ldots,
\]

one has

\[
\sqrt{\pi A_*}-\lambda_*
=0.239374260456223\ldots,
\]

so (161.5) gives coefficient

\[
3S_*+2(0.239374260456223\ldots)+c_*
=9.084984261160066\ldots.
\tag{161.6}
\]

The derived scalar `h/D>=1.8169968522...` is **not** promoted as an improvement: it is weaker than the unconditional audited §146 bound `h/D>=1.8195135379...`. The value of (161.5) is the retained `d_H,s_Z,G_D` structure and its applicability away from a scalar h-only projection.

## 161.2 Scope and frontier effect

- `G_D` is exact and nonnegative; no endpoint saturation is assumed in (161.3)–(161.4).
- The internal-face estimate is valid with bridges and articulation points because it uses occurrence-counted outer length and componentwise planar isoperimetry.
- The historical inference “near equality in Eppstein implies a doubled near-geodesic boundary” remains rejected; no such squaregraph stability is used here.
- Contact-isolated source points remain outside the unloaded positive-contact components and core rigidity matrices.
- The theorem does not address the Type-I inactive-container branch.

The next useful use of (161.4) is an **all-regime correlated optimization**: combine the actual complement-gap diameter moment, the strict-rhombus area of the same carrier, and the explicit Type-II defect variables instead of optimizing any one scalar in isolation.

**Disposition:** §§160–161 are audited-working proof-only Type-II theorems. C-II and Level I remain open.

